Qore optional TypeScript catalogue source package
================================================

This recipe builds the full catalogue, with its production JavaScript dependency
tree, as a separate Architecture: all package. The native V8 source package
continues to exclude ts/. The catalogue requires the V8 package containing the
immutable directory-map fix, structured optional-resource initialization errors,
and matching HubSpot presentation catalogs. Its versioned compatibility
fingerprints reject unreviewed schema changes before cache activation. Activate
it explicitly as described in README.Debian.

PUBLICATION GATE: the full catalogue has not been cleared for redistribution.
The schema-provenance.json file records pinned evidence and the remaining
missing/conflicting grants. Asana and Jira declare Apache-2.0; the original
DocuSign import has been matched to its MIT-licensed upstream revision. Their
notices are recorded separately in debian/copyright. The other eight schema
files are repository qualification inputs only: export-source.py omits them
from Debian sources, copy-schemas.js omits them from compiled distributions,
and build-catalogue.py rejects their presence in package inputs or payloads.
The pinned local operation/presentation contracts and compatibility fingerprints
contain no provider request/response schema documents. See README.Debian for
explicit download/import and private snapshots, including the existing HubSpot
cache. No redistribution grant for omitted schemas is assumed.

The Facebook SDK's Platform License permits redistribution for
Facebook integrations, but its PPA eligibility still needs confirmation under
https://documentation.ubuntu.com/launchpad/user/reference/launchpad-and-community/legal/launchpad-policies/.
A service-specific redistribution grant and Launchpad hosting eligibility are
separate requirements. Complete the per-file Debian
copyright mapping and preserve all applicable terms before uploading a source
or binary package to any public repository. These checks apply to testing PPAs
as well as general releases. No package upload is performed by this recipe.

Build from the .dsc and both upstream components: the first-party ts/ sources
plus the versioned orig-vendor tarball. The vendor component supplies Yarn
4.12.0, its license and the exact cache archives listed in vendor-manifest.json.
Do not substitute an existing node_modules directory, a different Yarn release
or a newly resolved dependency graph. The manifest validates package.json,
yarn.lock, the Yarn executable and every cache archive before and after build.

For a checkout, first export tracked, reviewed sources into a NEW directory:

 python3 ts/debian/export-source.py /absolute/path/qore-v8-app-catalogue-VERSION

Obtain the matching orig-vendor component from the qualified source package and
extract it in that exported directory (creating vendor/). Create the main orig
archive from the export excluding debian/ and vendor/, and retain the vendor
component separately. Do not archive ts/ directly: it retains historical schema
qualification inputs which are not cleared for this package. New source files
must be tracked before export. The vendor directory is not committed to Git.
Dependency updates require a newly prefetched and reviewed component,
updated integrity records and the same offline qualification. The Yarn download
is https://repo.yarnpkg.com/4.12.0/packages/yarnpkg-cli/bin/yarn.js; its expected
SHA-256 is in vendor-manifest.json and its packageManager SHA-512 is in package.json.
All network prefetching happens before creating the source package. Upstream npm
archives and their license/notice files are preserved in the vendor component.
The notice inventory also includes README files containing complete license
grants and separate copyright notices such as tslib's CopyrightNotice.txt.
These files are copied verbatim into the binary package's dependency-notices
directory. The separately reviewed notice-manifest.json binds this inventory
to the entire vendor manifest and checks every retained notice's SHA-256.
It also pins 43 supplemental upstream notices to the npm release's git revision
and includes Yarn's license. Supplemental notices are committed under
debian/upstream-notices; builds never fetch them. The binary package contains
an installed-notices.json digest inventory for verifying the installed bytes.
Debhelper compression excludes dependency-notices/ so the recorded paths and
digests describe the installed files directly; the .deb itself is compressed.
Missing, modified, unlisted or symlinked notice inputs fail the build.
Run `python3 debian/dependency_notices.py verify` in an exported source tree
with vendor/ present to check the notice inputs independently of Qore.

Notice retention does not establish complete license coverage. In particular,
42 dependency archives still have only embedded or metadata license evidence;
mixed-license generated code and nested bundled components require per-file
mapping before publication. The per-file review must cover development inputs
in the source package as well as the production dependency tree. Reviewed
license identifiers retain the original package metadata
in license_review; conflicting declarations remain explicit review findings.

Install declared Build-Depends, then run dpkg-buildpackage -b -us -uc from the
extracted source directory. The complete offline build is qualified on Ubuntu
26.04 amd64. The installed Architecture: all package also passes native Ubuntu
26.04 arm64 discovery and production tests, notice verification and
install/purge/reinstall checks. The notice verifier and installer are additionally
tested on Debian 13 amd64 and arm64; this alone does not qualify the complete
catalogue on Debian. Other build architectures have not been qualified. npm's
optional development bindings are platform dependent even though the installed
JavaScript package is architecture independent. No native executable or addon
is permitted in the installed catalogue tree.

The build disables Yarn network access, global cache reuse, telemetry and
third-party lifecycle scripts. Its cache/global data remain in the build tree,
so an inaccessible home directory is supported. It compiles the first-party
TypeScript and prunes schemas using the project's normal build, runs offline
Jest regressions and creates a production-only dependency tree. The map is
generated with Qore's own YAML serializer for byte-identical read-only loading.

The runtime tree excludes Rollup's unused native build binding, node-gyp added
by Yarn for evp_bytestokey's test:prepare script, KaTeX font-generation tools,
identified upstream test/release helpers and development/VCS metadata. It keeps
runtime code, generated fonts and upstream notices. KaTeX rendering, the JS
EVP helper, a mocked Contentful request and a real private CLI invocation test
these choices. CLI scripts are executable; type declarations and other data
remain non-executable. Private embedded libraries/fonts retain their upstream
relative layout; their maintenance and security updates remain this package's
responsibility.

Build tests can be disabled with the usual nocheck option/profile. The installed
runtime autopkgtest checks production-only dependencies and the exact qualified
app/action inventory in fresh Qore tiered and AST processes, using installed
modules and an unprivileged test user. Live service API operations require their
own credentials and are not covered by these offline tests. Supplement with
repeat-build comparison, source/payload parity, package inspection, Lintian,
HubSpot's native bridge regression and install/purge/reinstall qualification.
